<h2>Headless use</h2>
<p>
    The decoding pipeline has no dependency on Blazor or on a browser. It runs anywhere .NET
    runs, which is what makes it testable and benchmarkable.
</p>
<pre><code>using var decoder = new BarcodeDecoder(new ScannerOptions
{
    Formats = BarcodeFormat.QrCode | BarcodeFormat.DataMatrix,
    DuplicateSuppressionWindow = TimeSpan.Zero,
});

var result = decoder.Decode(rgbaPixels, width, height, PixelFormat.Rgba32);
if (result is not null)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{result.Format}: {result.Text}");
    Console.WriteLine($"took {decoder.LastFrame.Total.TotalMilliseconds:0.00} ms");
}</code></pre>

<h2>Extending the decoder</h2>
<p>
    A new symbology implements <code>IRowDecoder</code> for a linear code or
    <code>IMatrixDecoder</code> for a two dimensional one. Both receive already binarised input,
    so a new reader only has to contribute its own character table and structure.
</p>
<pre><code>public interface IRowDecoder
{
    BarcodeFormat Formats { get; }
    SymbolDecodeResult? DecodeRow(int rowNumber, BitRow row, BarcodeFormat enabledFormats);
}

public interface IMatrixDecoder
{
    BarcodeFormat Formats { get; }
    SymbolDecodeResult? Decode(BitMatrix image, MatrixDecodeOptions options);
}</code></pre>
<p>
    Binarisation is an extension point too: <code>IBinarizer</code> has one implementation tuned
    for matrix codes and one for linear rows, and a replacement can be substituted for either.
</p>
